  <!-- CHAPTER 1 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch1">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.01</span><h2>Introduction to the Linux Shell</h2></div>
    <p>When you open a Linux terminal, you interact with a program called the <strong>Shell</strong>. On Ubuntu, the default shell is <strong>Bash</strong> (Bourne Again Shell).</p>
    <p>Think of the shell as your personal assistant: you type commands, the shell understands them, and the operating system performs the requested task.</p>

    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div><span class="label">anatomy of a prompt</span></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-user">root</span><span class="prompt-at">@</span><span class="prompt-host">abc123</span><span class="prompt-sym">:~#</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table class="ref">
      <tr><th>Part</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>root</code></td><td>Current logged-in user</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>abc123</code></td><td>Computer hostname</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>~</code></td><td>Current directory (Home folder)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>#</code></td><td>Root user prompt</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>A normal user's prompt uses <code>$</code> instead of <code>#</code>:</p>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div><span class="label">normal user</span></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-user">sourabh</span><span class="prompt-at">@</span><span class="prompt-host">devserver</span><span class="prompt-sym">:~$</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="scenario">
      <span class="icon">🎬</span>
      <div class="content">
        <span class="tag">Live scenario</span>
        <p>You're a DevOps Engineer connecting to an Azure Virtual Machine. You see <code>azureadmin@prod-web01:~$</code> and instantly know: logged-in user is <code>azureadmin</code>, machine is <code>prod-web01</code>, you're in the home folder, and you're a normal user — not root.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 2 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch2">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.02</span><h2>Your First Linux Commands</h2></div>
    <p class="lede">These commands are completely safe — they only display information.</p>

    <h3 style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace; font-size:16px; margin:26px 0 8px;">1. <code>whoami</code> — shows the current user</h3>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> whoami</p>
        <p class="line out">root</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Real world</span><p>You SSH into a production server. Before deleting anything, verify who you are. If it shows <code>devuser</code> instead of <code>root</code>, you can't perform administrative tasks.</p></div></div>

    <h3 style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace; font-size:16px; margin:26px 0 8px;">2. <code>hostname</code> — shows the machine's name</h3>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> hostname</p>
        <p class="line out">db01</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>Your company runs <code>web01</code>, <code>web02</code>, <code>db01</code>, <code>db02</code>, <code>backup01</code>. Before restarting a service, run <code>hostname</code> to confirm you're on the database server, not the web server.</p></div></div>

    <h3 style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace; font-size:16px; margin:26px 0 8px;">3. <code>date</code> — shows current date and time</h3>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> date</p>
        <p class="line out">Tue Jul 28 10:15:20 UTC 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>An app stopped working at 10:00 AM. If <code>date</code> shows a mismatched time like 8:01 AM, the server clock is wrong — which can break logs, certificates, and scheduled tasks.</p></div></div>

    <h3 style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace; font-size:16px; margin:26px 0 8px;">4. <code>uname -a</code> — kernel and system info</h3>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> uname -a</p>
        <p class="line out">Linux prod-web01 6.8.0 x86_64 GNU/Linux</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <table class="ref">
      <tr><th>Part</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Linux</code></td><td>Kernel name</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>prod-web01</code></td><td>Hostname</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>6.8.0</code></td><td>Kernel version</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>x86_64</code></td><td>Architecture</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GNU/Linux</code></td><td>Operating system</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>A vendor asks if your server is 32-bit or 64-bit. Running <code>uname -a</code> and seeing <code>x86_64</code> confirms it's 64-bit.</p></div></div>

    <h3 style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace; font-size:16px; margin:26px 0 8px;">5. <code>echo</code> — prints text</h3>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> echo "Deployment Started"</p>
        <p class="line out">Deployment Started</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>Before writing a shell script, test a message with <code>echo</code> to confirm your syntax works.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 3 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch3">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.03</span><h2>Command Flags</h2></div>
    <p>Flags change the behavior of a command.</p>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> uname</p>
        <p class="line out">Linux</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> uname -s <span style="color:var(--muted);">   # explicit form</span></p>
        <p class="line out">Linux</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> uname -a <span style="color:var(--muted);">   # detailed info</span></p>
        <p class="line out">Linux prod-web01 6.8.0 x86_64 GNU/Linux</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>During troubleshooting, support asks only for the kernel name. Use <code>uname -s</code> instead of <code>uname -a</code> since they only need <code>Linux</code>.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 4 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch4">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.04</span><h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2></div>
    <table class="ref">
      <tr><th>Shortcut</th><th>Action</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Ctrl + C</code></td><td>Stops a running command</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Ctrl + L</code></td><td>Clears the terminal screen (same as <code>clear</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>↑</code> Up Arrow</td><td>Recalls previous commands</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>You accidentally started an endless process like <code>ping google.com</code>. Instead of closing the terminal, press <code>Ctrl + C</code>. Instead of retyping <code>sudo systemctl restart nginx</code>, press <code>↑</code> and hit Enter.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 5 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch5">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.05</span><h2><code style="color:var(--green);">pwd</code> — Print Working Directory</h2></div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> pwd</p>
        <p class="line out">/root</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>Before saving a configuration file, run <code>pwd</code>. If it shows <code>/var/www/html</code>, you know the file will land in the web server directory.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 6 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch6">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.06</span><h2><code style="color:var(--green);">ls</code> — List Files and Folders</h2></div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> ls <span style="color:var(--muted);"># current folder</span></p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> ls / <span style="color:var(--muted);"># root directory</span></p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> ls /etc <span style="color:var(--muted);"># configuration folder</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>A web application seems missing. Running <code>ls /var/www/html</code> reveals <code>index.html</code>, <code>css</code>, <code>images</code>, <code>js</code> — confirming the app files exist.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 7 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch7">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.07</span><h2><code style="color:var(--green);">cat</code> — Display File Contents</h2></div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cat /etc/hostname</p>
        <p class="line out">prod-web01</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cat /etc/os-release</p>
        <p class="line out">Ubuntu 24.04 LTS</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>A support engineer asks which Linux version you're using. Run <code>cat /etc/os-release</code> — no guessing required.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 8 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch8">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.08</span><h2>Tab Completion</h2></div>
    <p>Instead of typing <code>cat /etc/os-release</code> in full, type <code>cat /et</code> and press <strong>TAB</strong> — it becomes <code>cat /etc/</code>. Press <strong>TAB</strong> again to complete the filename.</p>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>For a long directory name like <code>terraform-production-environment-backup</code>, just type <code>cd ter</code>, press <strong>Tab</strong>, and Bash auto-completes it (or shows matches). Saves time and avoids typos.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 9 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch9">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.09</span><h2><code style="color:var(--green);">mkdir</code> — Create Directories</h2></div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> mkdir notes</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> mkdir -p /root/projects/api/src <span style="color:var(--muted);"># nested dirs</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>Starting a new Terraform project: <code>mkdir -p /root/projects/terraform/modules/network</code> creates every missing directory in one command.</p></div></div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 10 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch10">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.10</span><h2><code style="color:var(--green);">cd</code> — Change Directories</h2></div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd /etc</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:6px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd notes <span style="color:var(--muted);"># move into a folder</span></p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:6px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd .. <span style="color:var(--muted);"># go back one level</span></p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:6px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd ~ <span style="color:var(--muted);"># go home</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="scenario"><span class="icon">🎬</span><div class="content"><span class="tag">Live scenario</span><p>Editing Nginx config: <code>cd /etc/nginx</code>. When you're done, just type <code>cd</code> to return home.</p></div></div>

    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:22px;">Typical workflow:</p>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> mkdir project</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd project</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> pwd</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> ls</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CHAPTER 11 -->
  <section class="chapter" id="ch11">
    <div class="chapter-head"><span class="num">CH.11</span><h2>Redirecting Output (<code style="color:var(--green);">&gt;</code>)</h2></div>
    <p>Instead of printing to the screen, redirect output into a file.</p>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> whoami > user.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> hostname > host.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cat host.txt</p>
        <p class="line out">prod-web01</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="scenario">
      <span class="icon">🎬</span>
      <div class="content">
        <span class="tag">Live scenario — server audit</span>
        <p>Collecting system details for documentation, attachable to a support ticket:</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cmdcard">
      <div class="titlebar"><div class="dots"><span class="dot red"></span><span class="dot yellow"></span><span class="dot green"></span></div><span class="label">/root/answers</span></div>
      <div class="body">
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> mkdir -p /root/answers</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cd /root/answers</p>
        <p class="line in" style="margin-top:10px;"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> whoami > user.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> hostname > host.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> uname -s > kernel.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> ls / > root-listing.txt</p>
        <p class="line in"><span class="prompt-sym">$</span> cat /etc/os-release > os.txt</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--muted); font-size:14.5px;">Five files, zero manual copy-pasting — ready to share with the operations team.</p>
  </section>

